+\subsection{Research Track: Covariant Two-Form Vortex-Worldsheet Sector}
+\label{sec:rt_covariant_two_form_worldsheet}
+
+\subsubsection{Status and scope}
+
+\textbf{[ORTHODOX STRUCTURAL TEMPLATE / RESEARCH-TRACK SST IDENTIFICATION / NOT CANONIZED].}
+A two-form gauge field is the standard covariant field that couples electrically
+to a string worldsheet \cite{KalbRamond1974}.  Effective worldsheet descriptions
+of thin vortex lines are also standard in fluid and superfluid EFTs
+\cite{HornNicolisPenco2015}.  The SST-specific proposal is only that a resolved,
+closed swirl string may admit such a dual long-wavelength representation after
+its finite core has been integrated out.  This section does not identify the
+worldsheet charge with \(\Gamma_0\), does not replace the finite-core Euler or
+Biot--Savart description, and does not promote a new fundamental field.
+
+\subsubsection{Form degree, gauge symmetry, and action}
+
+Let
+\begin{align}
+    B&\in\Omega^2(M),
+    &
+    H&=dB\in\Omega^3(M),
+    &
+    H_{\mu\nu\rho}&=3\nabla_{[\mu}B_{\nu\rho]},
+    \label{eq:rt_two_form_definitions}
+\end{align}
+with gauge transformation
+\begin{align}
+    B\mapsto B+d\Lambda,
+    \qquad
+    \Lambda\in\Omega^1(M).
+    \label{eq:rt_two_form_gauge}
+\end{align}
+For a closed worldsheet \(X^\mu(\sigma^a)\), \(a=0,1\), the minimal
+source-coupled action is
+\begin{align}
+    S_{B\Sigma}
+    ={}&
+    -\frac{1}{12g_B^2}
+    \int_M d^4x\sqrt{-g}\,
+    H_{\mu\nu\rho}H^{\mu\nu\rho}
+    +
+    \frac{q_B}{2}
+    \int_\Sigma d^2\sigma\,
+    \epsilon^{ab}
+    B_{\mu\nu}(X)
+    \partial_aX^\mu\partial_bX^\nu,
+    \label{eq:rt_two_form_worldsheet_action}
+\end{align}
+where the sign of the kinetic term is chosen for positive field energy in the
+\((-+++ )\) convention.  A separate geometric tension term
+\(-T_\Sigma\int_\Sigma\sqrt{-\gamma}\,d^2\sigma\) may be used only after
+\(T_\Sigma\) has been matched to the resolved finite-core energy; it is not a
+new independent particle-mass parameter.
+
+The antisymmetric worldsheet current is
+\begin{align}
+    J_\Sigma^{\mu\nu}(x)
+    =
+    q_B\int_\Sigma d^2\sigma\,
+    \epsilon^{ab}
+    \partial_aX^\mu\partial_bX^\nu
+    \frac{\delta^{(4)}(x-X(\sigma))}{\sqrt{-g}},
+    \label{eq:rt_worldsheet_current}
+\end{align}
+and variation gives
+\begin{align}
+    \nabla_\mu H^{\mu\nu\rho}
+    &=g_B^2J_\Sigma^{\nu\rho},
+    &
+    \nabla_{[\sigma}H_{\mu\nu\rho]}&=0.
+    \label{eq:rt_two_form_field_equations}
+\end{align}
+For a closed worldsheet,
+\begin{align}
+    \nabla_\mu J_\Sigma^{\mu\nu}=0.
+    \label{eq:rt_worldsheet_current_conservation}
+\end{align}
+An open worldsheet requires an endpoint current or an explicit boundary/defect
+sector; otherwise Eq.~\eqref{eq:rt_worldsheet_current_conservation} is violated.
+
+\subsubsection{Charge-cycle and degree guard}
+
+Because \(*H\in\Omega^1(M)\) in \(3+1\) dimensions, the electric string charge
+is measured on a one-cycle \(C\) linking the worldsheet,
+\begin{align}
+    Q_B[C]
+    =
+    \frac{1}{g_B^2}\oint_C {*H}.
+    \label{eq:rt_two_form_electric_charge}
+\end{align}
+Equivalently, if \(C=\partial D\), Stokes' theorem applies to the two-form
+\(d{*H}\) on \(D\).  The inherited expression \(\int_{S^2}H\) is rejected:
+a three-form cannot be integrated over a two-surface.  An \(H\)-flux, when a
+suitable cycle exists, is a three-cycle integral.
+
+\subsubsection{Finite-core matching and validity regime}
+
+Let \(a\) denote the resolved tube radius and \(\mathcal R\) the smallest local
+curvature or background-variation scale.  The worldsheet reduction is admissible
+only in a controlled slender-core regime,
+\begin{align}
+    \epsilon_{\rm ws}
+    :=
+    \max\!\left(
+        \frac{a}{\mathcal R},
+        \frac{a}{\operatorname{Reach}(K)},
+        ka
+    \right)
+    \ll1,
+    \label{eq:rt_worldsheet_slenderness_gate}
+\end{align}
+with resolved circulation, finite reach, and no unresolved reconnection during
+the comparison interval.  The matching problem is to derive
+\begin{align}
+    \bigl(g_B,q_B,T_\Sigma\bigr)
+    =
+    \mathcal M_{\rm ws}
+    \bigl[
+hoSub,\rhoF,\Jomega,\muell,\Gamma_0,\rc;
+    \text{core profile and boundary data}\bigr]
+    \label{eq:rt_worldsheet_matching_map}
+\end{align}
+without inserting the target particle mass or setting \(q_B=\Gamma_0\) by
+notation.
+
+\subsubsection{Reconnection and promotion gates}
+
+A smooth conserved worldsheet current describes topology-preserving evolution.
+Reconnection, phase slip, or nucleation lies outside that closed-current sector
+and must be represented by a defect current, a boundary term, or a breakdown of
+the worldsheet approximation.  Promotion requires all of the following:
+\begin{enumerate}
+    \item convergence of resolved finite-core observables to the worldsheet model
+    as \(\epsilon_{\rm ws}\to0\);
+    \item an independently derived matching map
+    Eq.~\eqref{eq:rt_worldsheet_matching_map};
+    \item correct Kelvin/helicity bookkeeping in topology-preserving runs;
+    \item an explicit failure or defect law during reconnection;
+    \item no extra mass or charge parameter hidden in \(T_\Sigma\) or \(q_B\);
+    \item a demonstrated advantage over the existing centreline/Biot--Savart
+    description on held-out configurations.
+\end{enumerate}
+
+\subsection{Research Track: Parity-Sensitive Helicity Response}
+\label{sec:rt_parity_sensitive_helicity_response}
+
+\subsubsection{Operator and dimensional audit}
+
+\textbf{[BRIDGE/RESEARCH / NOT A WEAK-INTERACTION DERIVATION].}
+For a local resolved velocity \(\mathbf u\) and vorticity
+\(\boldsymbol\omega=\nabla\times\mathbf u\), define the kinetic-helicity
+pseudoscalar
+\begin{align}
+    \mathcal P_h
+    :=
+    \mathbf u\cdot\boldsymbol\omega.
+    \label{eq:rt_kinetic_helicity_pseudoscalar}
+\end{align}
+A dimensionally admissible local response-density operator is
+\begin{align}
+    \mathcal O_h^{(\ell)}
+    :=
+    r_c\,\rho_{\rm resp}^{(\ell)}\,
+    \mathbf u\cdot\boldsymbol\omega,
+    \qquad
+    [\mathcal O_h^{(\ell)}]=\mathrm{J\,m^{-3}},
+    \label{eq:rt_helicity_response_operator}
+\end{align}
+where \(\rho_{\rm resp}^{(\ell)}\) is the response coefficient at the explicitly
+stated coarse-graining scale.  It may approach \(\rhoF\) only in the guarded
+quasi-static sector; it must not be silently identified with \(\rhoSub\),
+\(\rhoM\), or \(\rhocore\).
+
+Under spatial parity, \(\mathbf u\) is polar and
+\(\boldsymbol\omega\) is axial, so
+\begin{align}
+    \mathcal P_h\xrightarrow{P}-\mathcal P_h.
+\end{align}
+Under time reversal both \(\mathbf u\) and \(\boldsymbol\omega\) reverse, so
+\(\mathcal P_h\) is time-reversal even.  Therefore
+\begin{align}
+    \mathcal L_{\rm PV}
+    &=g_5\,\mathcal O_h^{(\ell)}\,\mathcal S_5,
+    \qquad \mathcal S_5\ \text{parity even},
+    \label{eq:rt_parity_odd_response_term}
+\end{align}
+is parity odd, whereas
+\begin{align}
+    \mathcal L_{\rm align}
+    &=g_\chi\,\mathcal O_h^{(\ell)}\,\chi_K,
+    \qquad \chi_K\xrightarrow{P}-\chi_K,
+    \label{eq:rt_chiral_alignment_term}
+\end{align}
+is parity even and represents helicity--chirality alignment.  The two terms must
+not be conflated.  In particular, multiplying one pseudoscalar by another can
+split alignment sectors without deriving fundamental weak parity violation.
+
+\subsubsection{Canonical-scale benchmark and density guard}
+
+Using the quasi-static numerical value only as a dimensional benchmark and the
+turnover estimate \(\omega_c=\vchar/\rc\),
+\begin{align}
+    r_c\rhoF\vchar\omega_c
+    =
+    \rhoF\vchar{}^2
+    =
+    8.37548784\times10^5\ \mathrm{J\,m^{-3}}.
+    \label{eq:rt_helicity_response_numeric_scale}
+\end{align}
+Equation~\eqref{eq:rt_helicity_response_numeric_scale} is not a predicted weak
+energy density: \(g_5\), the resolved field amplitude, the support volume, and
+the scale-dependent response are unfixed, and \(\rhoF\) is not canonically valid
+as a microscopic core density.
+
+\subsubsection{Symmetry tests and falsifiers}
+
+For mirror-related chiral states in an unbiased parity-symmetric background,
+the chirality-blind rest functional must satisfy
+\begin{align}
+    M_0(K)=M_0(\bar K).
+    \label{eq:rt_chiral_mass_degeneracy_gate}
+\end{align}
+A controlled helicity background may produce opposite first-order response,
+\begin{align}
+    \delta E_{\rm align}(K)
+    =
+    -\delta E_{\rm align}(\bar K),
+    \label{eq:rt_chiral_alignment_mirror_split}
+\end{align}
+while an ideally amphichiral state must have vanishing parity-odd ensemble
+average to first order.  Required tests are:
+\begin{enumerate}
+    \item mirror-pair simulations with identical geometry, circulation, reach,
+    and boundary conditions;
+    \item reversal of background helicity and independent reversal of knot
+    chirality;
+    \item separation of parity-odd linear response from parity-even quadratic
+    deformation;
+    \item verification that any claimed \(V-A\) limit follows from field
+    representations and projectors, not from the sign of
+    \(\mathbf u\cdot\boldsymbol\omega\) alone;
+    \item rejection if the effect survives when the helicity pseudoscalar is
+    nulled or if it changes a chirality-blind mass calibration at zero bias.
+\end{enumerate}
+
+\subsection{Research Track: Gauge-Emergence Certification Ladder}
+\label{sec:rt_gauge_emergence_certification_ladder}
+
+\subsubsection{Status and target discipline}
+
+\textbf{[RESEARCH PROGRAMME / NO PREASSIGNED ALGEBRA].}
+The Standard-Model algebra may be used as the comparison target, but it may not
+be assumed during construction of the coarse variables or inserted through a
+charge table.  The input to the programme is a scale-dependent set of
+independently defined currents or holonomies \(J_A^{(\ell)}\), their Poisson or
+commutator algebra, and the actual constraints and quotient symmetries of the
+substrate model.
+
+\subsubsection{Gate G0: physical mode census}
+
+Before naming a Lie algebra, count the independent local physical modes after
+constraints and gauge redundancies.  For example,
+\begin{align}
+    \dim\!\left[SO(3)^3/SO(3)_{\rm diag}\right]=6
+    \neq
+    12=
+    \dim\!\left[\mathfrak{su}(3)\oplus\mathfrak{su}(2)\oplus\mathfrak u(1)\right].
+    \label{eq:rt_gauge_mode_count_guard}
+\end{align}
+Thus three directors modulo a common rotation are insufficient as a standalone
+mode-count derivation.  Additional independent variables must be physically
+identified rather than inferred from the desired answer.
+
+\subsubsection{Gate G1: nontrivial curvature and locality}
+
+If a proposed connection is defined solely by
+\begin{align}
+    \mathcal W_\mu=(\partial_\mu O)O^{-1},
+\end{align}
+then its curvature vanishes locally away from singularities by the
+Maurer--Cartan identity.  A nontrivial field strength must therefore descend
+from independent link variables, unresolved defects, nontrivial bundle
+patching, or holonomy that cannot be globally gauged away.  The location and
+physical energy of those structures must be explicit.
+
+\subsubsection{Gate G2: closure and Jacobi residuals}
+
+Let \(\Pi_\ell\) project onto the candidate generator span.  Define
+\begin{align}
+    \mathcal R_{\rm close}(\ell)
+    :=
+    \max_{A,B}
+    \frac{
+        \left\|
+        (1-\Pi_\ell)\{J_A^{(\ell)},J_B^{(\ell)}\}
+        \right\|
+    }{
+        \left\|
+        \{J_A^{(\ell)},J_B^{(\ell)}\}
+        \right\|+\varepsilon
+    },
+    \label{eq:rt_gauge_closure_residual}
+\end{align}
+and
+\begin{align}
+    \mathcal R_{\rm Jac}(\ell)
+    :=
+    \max_{A,B,C}
+    \frac{
+        \left\|
+        \{J_A,\{J_B,J_C\}\}
+        +\{J_B,\{J_C,J_A\}\}
+        +\{J_C,\{J_A,J_B\}\}
+        \right\|
+    }{
+        \mathcal N_{ABC}+\varepsilon
+    }.
+    \label{eq:rt_gauge_jacobi_residual}
+\end{align}
+Promotion requires convergence of both residuals under scale, resolution,
+boundary-condition, and coarse-graining-kernel changes.  A least-squares fit to
+preselected Standard-Model structure constants is not a closure proof.
+
+\subsubsection{Gate G3: representation and charge separation}
+
+Each proposed particle sector must furnish a representation of the derived
+algebra.  The state descriptor must separate all states assigned different
+charges.  In particular, no linear charge map is admissible when two states
+have identical input invariants but are assigned distinct hypercharges.  The
+map must predict charge conjugation, left/right multiplet structure, and family
+replication without using the observed charge table as a lookup function.
+
+\subsubsection{Gate G4: anomaly and global-consistency tests}
+
+After all fields are written as left-handed Weyl representations, the derived
+content must satisfy at least
+\begin{align}
+    \sum_i T_{SU(3)}(R_i)Y_i&=0,
+    &
+    \sum_i T_{SU(2)}(R_i)Y_i&=0,
+    \\
+    \sum_i Y_i^3&=0,
+    &
+    \sum_i Y_i&=0,
+    \label{eq:rt_gauge_anomaly_gates}
+\end{align}
+for the \([SU(3)]^2U(1)\), \([SU(2)]^2U(1)\), \([U(1)]^3\), and mixed
+gravity--\(U(1)\) anomalies, together with the global \(SU(2)\) condition that the number of left-handed doublets is even.  Anomalies must vanish from independently derived representations;
+``cancellation by construction'' after fitting the charge table has no
+promotion force \cite{Weinberg1967,PDG2024}.
+
+\subsubsection{Gate G5: couplings, running, and universality}
+
+Effective couplings must be extracted from normalized two- and three-point
+functions or response coefficients with uncertainty propagation.  The inferred
+algebra and coupling flow must be stable under admissible coarse-graining
+kernels and must possess a common infrared limit.  Qualitative resemblance of
+a beta-function sign is insufficient; thresholds, normalization conventions,
+and the number of active modes must be stated.
+
+\subsubsection{Gate G6: out-of-sample phenomenology}
+
+Only after G0--G5 close may the model be tested against electroweak and strong
+observables.  At minimum the held-out suite must include charge assignments,
+weak multiplets, parity structure, running couplings, neutral-current ratios,
+confinement/deconfinement behaviour, and a spectrum not used to choose the
+algebra.  Failure at any earlier gate returns the construction to a generic
+multi-director or link-field model rather than a Standard-Model derivation.
+
+\subsubsection{Promotion certificate}
+
+The minimal certificate is the vector
+\begin{align}
+    \mathcal C_{\rm gauge}
+    =
+    \Bigl(
+        N_{\rm phys},
+        \mathcal R_{\rm curvature},
+        \mathcal R_{\rm close},
+        \mathcal R_{\rm Jac},
+        \mathcal R_{\rm rep},
+        \mathcal R_{\rm charge},
+        \mathcal R_{\rm anomaly},
+        \mathcal R_{\beta},
+        \mathcal R_{\rm holdout}
+    \Bigr).
+    \label{eq:rt_gauge_promotion_certificate}
+\end{align}
+No item may be omitted by replacing it with the target algebra name.
+
